{"name":"Europe Blue","hex":"#006796","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Europe","blocks":[{"text":"Europe is a continent located entirely in the Northern Hemisphere and mostly in the Eastern Hemisphere. It is bordered by the Arctic Ocean to the north, the Atlantic Ocean to the west, the Mediterranean Sea to the south, and Asia to the east. Europe shares the landmass of Eurasia with Asia, and of Afro-Eurasia with both Africa and Asia. Europe is commonly considered to be separated from Asia by the watershed of the Ural Mountains, the Ural River, the Caspian Sea, the Greater Caucasus, the Black Sea, and the Turkish straits."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Europe_orthographic_Caucasus_Urals_boundary_%28with_borders%29.svg/330px-Europe_orthographic_Caucasus_Urals_boundary_%28with_borders%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europe"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Possible_definitions_of_the_boundary_between_Europe_and_Asia.png/500px-Possible_definitions_of_the_boundary_between_Europe_and_Asia.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Possible_definitions_of_the_boundary_between_Europe_and_Asia.png/500px-Possible_definitions_of_the_boundary_between_Europe_and_Asia.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Definitions used for the boundary between Asia and Europe in different periods of history. B (Urals) and F (Caucasus) form the modern conception.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APossible_definitions_of_the_boundary_between_Europe_and_Asia.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Europa_Prima_Pars_Terrae_in_Forma_Virginis.jpg/500px-Europa_Prima_Pars_Terrae_in_Forma_Virginis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Europa_Prima_Pars_Terrae_in_Forma_Virginis.jpg/1280px-Europa_Prima_Pars_Terrae_in_Forma_Virginis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Europa_Prima_Pars_Terrae_in_Forma_Virginis.jpg/500px-Europa_Prima_Pars_Terrae_in_Forma_Virginis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Depiction of Europa regina ('Queen Europe') in 1582","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEuropa_Prima_Pa